allenk@ugcs.caltech.edu (Allen Knutson) writes:

>ratner@ficus.cs.ucla.edu (Dave "Van Damme" Ratner) writes:

>>You can only write a scroll if you've
>>seen that scroll before; similarly, you can only write a spellbook if you've
>>seen that spellbook before.

>From something I found in the code, it looked like there's a 1 in 15 chance
>of writing something even if you haven't seen it before - 1 in 3 for wizards.
>I may have misinterpreted. Doesn't seem like it'd be too useful for non-
>wizards given the odds, but might be nice for wizards.

>Just tried it in discovery; was able to write identify and not genocide,
>having seen neither (as a wizard).				Allen K.

Yes, I agree that there is a 1-15 chance on writing an unknown scroll/spellbook
(1-3 for wizards), but the down side is that you get charged for the scroll
or spellbook regards of whether or not the write succeeds!  So at 1-15,
you could empty your pen and never get anything useful.  Even at 1-3, you'll
probably only get 1 or 2 scrolls out of it.  Not really worth the risk IMHO.

